#!/usr/bin/env bash
# fusion-protected-paths — the EFFECTIVE protected-path list the session is
# running under, each entry's origin layer, and whether the guard is actually
# enforcing it here. Read at Setup (agents/orchestrator.md Setup Step 5,
# skills/setup/SKILL.md Step 3) so a project SEES what it inherits instead of
# discovering it by being blocked.
#
# Usage:
#   fusion-protected-paths
#
# Output on stdout, one KEY=value per line, then one line per effective entry:
#
#   summary=8 protected paths resolved from the fusion plugin's own …
#   enforced=none                 # both | write-tools | measurement | none
#   enforced_write_tools=no
#   write_tools_dir=…             # the directory THAT half asks about (cwd)
#   write_tools_standdown=fusion-plugin-repo    # only when it is not enforcing
#   enforced_measurement=no
#   measurement_dir=…             # the directory THAT half asks about (the
#                                 # workbench root) — a different question, on
#                                 # purpose; see the note below
#   measurement_standdown=fusion-plugin-repo
#   guard_enabled=true
#   source=plugin                 # project | plugin | default
#   project_config=absent         # present | absent
#   project_config_path=…
#   entries=8
#   diagnostics=0
#   diagnostic=…                  # one line each, when the loader dropped something
#   path=agents/** origin=plugin  # origin: project | plugin | default | floor
#
# `summary` is first and is a plain sentence carrying the whole answer, so a
# caller can relay it without a paragraph explaining how to read the keys.
#
# Exit codes:
#   0  reported. An EMPTY effective list is a report, not a failure.
#   1  usage error.
#   2  no fusion workbench above the working directory; there is no project
#      layer to merge, so a project that narrowed the list would be shown the
#      plugin's. Nothing is printed.
#   3  the plugin's compiled hooks are missing, so nothing could be asked. Its
#      own code and not 2: "this project protects nothing" is an answer about
#      the project, and a broken install must never be reported as one.
#
# Every non-zero exit prints NO list at all, and says on stderr that no report
# was taken. That distinction is the point of the helper: "not measured" and
# "nothing is protected" must never look alike, because one of them is the
# state a project would act on.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why the list is read rather than assumed
#
# It was assumed. The shipped list — agents/**, rules/**, .claude/rules/**,
# hooks/config.json, hooks/hooks.json, settings.json, bin/monitor,
# .claude-plugin/plugin.json — names things in FUSION's own repository, and the
# plugin layer is the layer every consuming project inherits. Nothing reported
# it: the seeded fusion-guard.json deliberately does not restate the list and
# points at a file inside the install. The largest consuming project ran 143
# days under it, matching its own engineering documentation with rules/**, and
# produced 53 records of work that existed only because agents could not write
# files that project owned, with zero halts to show for it. Issue
# 260812-0843_*_the-guard-and-its-configuration-must-be-simplified-project-settable-and-defaulted-to-fit-or-not-shipped-to-consumers-at-all.md
#
# This helper decides nothing. Whether a consumer should inherit any protected
# path at all is the open question in that record, and this report is built to
# hold whichever way it is answered.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why enforcement is two lines and two directories
#
# The write-tool deny (hooks/guard.ts) stands down on isFusionPluginCwd(), which
# reads the WORKING DIRECTORY with no upward walk. The protected-path
# measurement (hooks/tracker.ts, via measurementRoot() in
# hooks/lib/protected-snapshot.ts) stands down on isFusionPluginRoot(root), which
# reads the WORKBENCH ROOT. The difference is deliberate and documented in
# hooks/lib/self-detect.ts: moving the measurement's root up without moving its
# stand-down up would revert a fusion developer's own edits whenever the session
# started in a subdirectory, and fusion-workbench/ makes that the ordinary case.
#
# So the two halves can disagree, and in fusion's own repository they do: from
# the root both stand down, from fusion-workbench/ the write-tool deny is active
# and the measurement is not. One collapsed "enforced" would print a falsehood
# there, so both are printed, each beside the directory it was answered for.
#
# The work is hooks/dist/protected-paths.js, resolved relative to this script
# rather than through $FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT, so an install copy and a work tree
# each run their own build. node is already a hard requirement — the guard hooks
# are node programs (hooks/hooks.json).

set -euo pipefail

here="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
entry="$here/../hooks/dist/protected-paths.js"

if [ ! -f "$entry" ]; then
  echo "fusion-protected-paths: $entry is missing — the plugin's compiled hooks are not installed, so no protected-path report was taken. The effective list is UNKNOWN here, not empty." >&2
  exit 3
fi

exec node "$entry" "$@"
